# AXIS CELL: predicate IDENTITY = `elsebranches_have_uncond`, the SIBLING of
# `match_has_catch_all`. The two answer the identical question -- "can control
# fall out of this construct without entering a branch?" -- for `if`/`elif`
# chains and for `match` respectively, and `elsebranches_have_uncond` has THREE
# `safety_commit` consumers of its own (the `SIf`, `SElif` and `EIf` joins) plus
# an inlined twin in `live_stmt_diverges`'s `SIf` arm. Before this cell, every
# artefact in the net watched `match_has_catch_all` and NOTHING watched the
# sibling, so a weakening of the sibling was invisible to all of them.
#
# `c` is false, so the `if` body never runs, the join is reached with `s` still
# moved, and `print(s)` is a use-after-move -- rejected by `gg check` with
# `error[E_UseAfterMove]`.
#
# RED-VERIFY (weaken the sibling's tail): `elsebranches_have_uncond` has exactly
# ONE `return false`; change it to `return true` and rebuild the driver.
# Measured: this cell flips rc 1 -> rc 0, 3504 bytes of C, and it is the ONLY
# outcome cell that moves -- every other reject cell, both accept canaries and
# all eleven pre-existing driver cells stay GREEN. (The `tests/lints.rs` body pin
# ALSO fires here, because it covers both classifiers; that is belt-and-braces,
# not a substitute -- three of the nine measured weakenings leave the WHOLE
# lint green.)
void sink(String ^x):
    pass

void probe(bool c):
    String s = "hello"
    sink(^s)
    if c:
        s = "a"
    print(s)

void main():
    probe(false)
